<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Soppeng Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1905 CE (approx.) · capital Watansoppeng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bugis kingdom in South Sulawesi, ally of Bone and Wajo in the Tellumpoccoe alliance against Gowa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Soppeng was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> kingdoms that formed in the fertile valleys of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> from the 14th century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming and trade grew. Its chronicles, written in the Bugis script, describe a founding in which a heavenly being, a tomanurung, was invited to rule by the leaders of the local communities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soppeng was governed by a datu advised by a council of local chiefs, and its wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. In 1582 it joined Bone and Wajo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tellumpoccoe">Tellumpoccoe</a>, 'three powers', alliance against the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Gowa Sultanate</a>. Gowa forced it to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1609.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soppeng later sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-palakka">Arung Palakka of Bone</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> against Gowa in 1666 to 1669. It remained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ally under the Bone confederation until the Dutch conquered South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> in 1905 and brought its rulers under direct colonial control. It is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soppeng-regency">Soppeng Regency</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1300 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:12.04%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Watansoppeng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, earlier Bugis indigenous religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bugis</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soppeng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=soppeng-kingdom" aria-label="Soppeng Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8005 2340 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Soppeng Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8325 2596L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8325 2596h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8325 2596h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Watansoppeng. <a href="/atlas?year=1602&amp;focus=soppeng-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

